20:28:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s all about incredibly low power consumption and a tiny built-in energy storage system. Even though you never put them in direct sunlight, most solar calculators are designed to work on **ambient indoor light** – from lamps, ceiling lights, or even the dim glow near a window. Here’s how they manage to keep going for years: 1. **The solar cell trickle-charges a battery or capacitor.** Inside the calculator, the little photovoltaic panel isn’t directly powering the electronics. Instead, it continuously tops up a rechargeable battery (often a small lithium coin cell, or a supercapacitor). Even very weak light produces a tiny current; because it’s constant, over time it stores enough energy to run the calculator when you press keys. 2. **The calculator uses almost no power.** Modern calculator chips are astonishingly energy-frugal. They can run on **microwatts** – a tiny fraction of what an LED needs. Except for the occasional button press or a faint LCD refresh, they’re basically asleep. The liquid crystal display (LCD) itself draws very little current (nanoamps to microamps), so the stored charge lasts a long time between top-ups. 3. **Energy is buffered for dark periods.** If you put the calculator in a drawer for weeks, it doesn’t die immediately. The capacitor or battery holds enough charge to run the calculator for **hours to days of active use** (and months on standby). Once it’s exposed to light again – even artificial light – the solar cell slowly replenishes that reservoir. So the “secret” is that you don’t need direct sun. Artificial light delivers enough photons for the solar panel to trickle-charge the internal storage, and the electronics are so low-power that even a tiny trickle is enough to keep them alive indefinitely.
